1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,995
Six-point-seven scoped it: auto-approve refunds under fifty dollars, for a verified

2
00:00:04,995 --> 00:00:09,359
account, capped at three approvals a day, escalate everything else to a human.

3
00:00:09,359 --> 00:00:13,948
That single sentence is where this worked solution starts, because a bounded scope

4
00:00:13,948 --> 00:00:18,649
is what turns "let the agent handle refunds" from an unshippable idea into a feature

5
00:00:18,649 --> 00:00:21,335
six real artifacts can actually be built around.

6
00:00:21,335 --> 00:00:25,979
This lecture covers the first three: the PRD, the golden set, and the rubric behind

7
00:00:25,979 --> 00:00:28,218
it, the what-is-it and the does-it-work.

8
00:00:28,218 --> 00:00:32,918
The next lecture covers the other three: cost, risk, and metrics, on this exact same

9
00:00:32,918 --> 00:00:33,366
feature.

10
00:00:33,366 --> 00:00:37,955
Watch how much of this walkthrough is just reassembling things this course already

11
00:00:37,955 --> 00:00:42,599
built in earlier sections, not inventing new judgement from scratch, because that's

12
00:00:42,599 --> 00:00:45,789
exactly what you'll be doing with your own six artifacts.

13
00:00:45,789 --> 00:00:50,154
Treat this the way 11.1 asked you to: as one valid, defensible answer, not the

14
00:00:50,154 --> 00:00:50,546
answer.

15
00:00:50,546 --> 00:00:55,078
Your own capstone feature almost certainly isn't a refund agent, and it shouldn't

16
00:00:55,078 --> 00:00:55,694
need to be.

17
00:00:55,694 --> 00:01:00,171
What should transfer to your own package is the shape of the reasoning, not this

18
00:01:00,171 --> 00:01:01,010
specific scope.

19
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:06,506
Notice the objective doesn't say "automate refunds." It says exactly whose time it's

20
00:01:06,506 --> 00:01:11,335
trying to save and under what condition, which is the difference 3.7 spent a whole

21
00:01:11,335 --> 00:01:15,751
lecture on: a PRD that could describe any feature isn't actually a PRD yet.

22
00:01:15,751 --> 00:01:20,344
The scope row is 6.7's bounded version, word for word, because a proposal that

23
00:01:20,344 --> 00:01:24,466
specific is what let that lecture's checklist pass in the first place.

24
00:01:24,466 --> 00:01:29,236
Notice, too, that the out-of-scope row is doing real work, not just restating the

25
00:01:29,236 --> 00:01:30,591
in-scope row backwards.

26
00:01:30,591 --> 00:01:35,419
"The 4th request in a day" is a case an unbounded version would never have thought

27
00:01:35,419 --> 00:01:39,718
to name, and naming it is what makes the daily cap enforceable instead of

28
00:01:39,718 --> 00:01:40,483
aspirational.

29
00:01:40,483 --> 00:01:44,429
The threshold row is where this PRD commits to something checkable.

30
00:01:44,429 --> 00:01:49,081
"Zero wrongful auto-approvals" is a number the golden set on the next slide can

31
00:01:49,081 --> 00:01:53,909
actually test, unlike a vaguer goal like "should be accurate," which no eval could

32
00:01:53,909 --> 00:01:55,735
ever definitively pass or fail.

33
00:01:56,285 --> 00:01:58,763
Four rows are enough to carry this walkthrough.

34
00:01:58,763 --> 00:02:02,825
They wouldn't be enough to actually brief an engineering team, and treating a

35
00:02:02,825 --> 00:02:07,255
summary like this one as the finished artifact is a shortcut worth naming before you

36
00:02:07,255 --> 00:02:07,677
take it.

37
00:02:08,227 --> 00:02:12,781
Notice both cases run identical first two steps, and diverge only at step three.

38
00:02:12,781 --> 00:02:17,564
That's 6.1's loop made concrete: the agent doesn't know in advance which ending it's

39
00:02:17,564 --> 00:02:22,175
heading toward, it decides after observing what the order history actually shows.

40
00:02:22,175 --> 00:02:26,559
Notice, too, that step one never has access to the tool that issues a refund.

41
00:02:26,559 --> 00:02:31,057
That's 8.2's read-then-act separation, applied here rather than just described.

42
00:02:31,057 --> 00:02:34,814
The email-reading step can only ever produce a structured summary.

43
00:02:34,814 --> 00:02:39,312
Issuing the refund is a different call entirely, gated behind step two's check,

44
00:02:39,312 --> 00:02:43,866
which is exactly what keeps a crafted sentence inside an email from turning into

45
00:02:43,866 --> 00:02:45,233
money moving on its own.

46
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:49,673
This is also why the golden set on the next slide tests more than correctness.

47
00:02:49,673 --> 00:02:54,456
It tests whether the boundary between reading and acting actually held, under a case

48
00:02:54,456 --> 00:02:56,221
built specifically to probe it.

49
00:02:56,771 --> 00:03:00,414
What should the agent do when the data itself is inconsistent?

50
00:03:00,414 --> 00:03:05,114
The correct move is the same as any case a rubric can't resolve with confidence:

51
00:03:05,114 --> 00:03:09,756
escalate by default, because an agent that guesses when the data disagrees with

52
00:03:09,756 --> 00:03:14,221
itself is exactly the failure mode 6.1 warned this course to design against.

53
00:03:14,771 --> 00:03:19,272
Read the second row specifically, because it's the one most golden sets skip.

54
00:03:19,272 --> 00:03:24,066
Exactly fifty dollars is where an implementation's "under fifty" either means what

55
00:03:24,066 --> 00:03:28,800
the PRD says or quietly means "fifty or less," and the only way to catch that gap

56
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:33,477
before a customer does is to put the exact boundary value in the set on purpose.

57
00:03:33,477 --> 00:03:38,387
The last row is 8.2's exact injection sentence, reused here rather than paraphrased,

58
00:03:38,387 --> 00:03:43,180
because a golden set is precisely where that lecture's warning becomes a checkable

59
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:44,992
test instead of a hypothetical.

60
00:03:44,992 --> 00:03:49,786
If this row ever fails, meaning the agent acts on the embedded instruction instead

61
00:03:49,786 --> 00:03:52,358
of ignoring it, that's not a tuning problem.

62
00:03:52,358 --> 00:03:56,800
It's the read-then-act boundary from the previous slide failing in practice.

63
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,255
These six are a sample, not the whole set.

64
00:03:59,255 --> 00:04:03,873
A02 asks for fifty, and a reasonable split for this feature runs roughly thirty

65
00:04:03,873 --> 00:04:08,783
clean auto-approves covering different dollar amounts, and twenty escalations spread

66
00:04:08,783 --> 00:04:13,518
across every named reason on this table plus the ambiguous case from the previous

67
00:04:13,518 --> 00:04:13,869
slide.

68
00:04:13,869 --> 00:04:18,545
Weight it toward the boundary the same way 4.2 originally taught, not toward the

69
00:04:18,545 --> 00:04:21,234
easy middle these six rows already moved past.

70
00:04:21,784 --> 00:04:26,298
Ambiguity is precisely what the human review step exists to absorb, not something

71
00:04:26,298 --> 00:04:28,973
the golden set should try to resolve on its own.

72
00:04:29,523 --> 00:04:33,490
Here's the part a rubric for this feature actually has to do work on.

73
00:04:33,490 --> 00:04:38,032
The approve-or-escalate decision itself is close to a factual check, two raters

74
00:04:38,032 --> 00:04:42,746
looking at the same order history will nearly always agree on it, which makes it a

75
00:04:42,746 --> 00:04:45,160
weak test of whether a rubric is any good.

76
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:47,977
The logged reason is where real judgement enters.

77
00:04:47,977 --> 00:04:52,806
A human picking up an escalated case reads that reason to decide what to do next, so

78
00:04:52,806 --> 00:04:57,463
"right decision, wrong reason" is a genuine failure, not a pass with an asterisk.

79
00:04:57,463 --> 00:05:02,292
Getting two raters to agree on what counts as an adequate reason, specific enough to

80
00:05:02,292 --> 00:05:06,948
act on, honest about the actual cause, is the calibration work 4.3 actually asked

81
00:05:06,948 --> 00:05:11,260
you to practise, applied here to something narrower than a fully open-ended

82
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:11,777
response.

83
00:05:11,777 --> 00:05:16,319
Notice this is a smaller, more tractable version of inter-rater work than 4.3's

84
00:05:16,319 --> 00:05:20,113
original example, precisely because the decision space is bounded.

85
00:05:20,113 --> 00:05:21,550
That's not a coincidence.

86
00:05:21,550 --> 00:05:26,379
A feature this narrowly scoped makes every downstream artifact easier to build well,

87
00:05:26,379 --> 00:05:30,461
which is its own quiet argument for scoping tightly in the first place.

88
00:05:31,011 --> 00:05:35,534
Say the honest caveat, because treating every wrong answer as equally bad is a

89
00:05:35,534 --> 00:05:38,492
mistake this specific feature can't afford to make.

90
00:05:38,492 --> 00:05:43,189
Recall 1.7's original objection to an unbounded version of this exact feature: it

91
00:05:43,189 --> 00:05:48,060
needs to be right every single time, not usually, because "usually" means real money

92
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:50,032
leaving on a predictable schedule.

93
00:05:50,032 --> 00:05:52,584
The bounded scope narrowed the blast radius.

94
00:05:52,584 --> 00:05:54,382
It didn't remove the asymmetry.

95
00:05:54,382 --> 00:05:58,615
Zero tolerance for a wrongful approval, real tolerance for an unnecessary

96
00:05:58,615 --> 00:06:03,254
escalation, is how this golden set respects that asymmetry instead of pretending

97
00:06:03,254 --> 00:06:05,052
both error types cost the same.

98
00:06:05,052 --> 00:06:09,865
This is worth naming explicitly in your own capstone too, whatever your feature is.

99
00:06:09,865 --> 00:06:12,707
Almost no real feature has symmetric error costs.

100
00:06:12,707 --> 00:06:17,578
A golden set that scores every mistake the same way is quietly hiding that asymmetry

101
00:06:17,578 --> 00:06:19,144
rather than confronting it.

102
00:06:19,144 --> 00:06:23,900
This is also exactly the kind of answer 10.6 asked you to be able to produce live,

103
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:25,465
under a follow-up question.

104
00:06:25,465 --> 00:06:30,337
"Why zero tolerance on one side and not the other" has a specific, defensible answer

105
00:06:30,337 --> 00:06:35,150
here, the two error types cost genuinely different amounts, and a candidate who can

106
00:06:35,150 --> 00:06:39,905
say that instantly is demonstrating the actual skill this course was built around.

107
00:06:40,455 --> 00:06:44,899
That's the standard this artifact pair was actually built to, and the exercise is

108
00:06:44,899 --> 00:06:48,410
worth doing on your own feature's real boundary, not this one's.

109
00:06:48,410 --> 00:06:53,018
Most first attempts at a golden set cluster entirely around the easy, obvious cases,

110
00:06:53,018 --> 00:06:55,047
because they're the fastest to write.

111
00:06:55,047 --> 00:06:59,655
The boundary cases, the exact cap, the unverified account, the embedded instruction,

112
00:06:59,655 --> 00:07:04,099
are where a golden set either earns its keep or turns out to have been decorative

113
00:07:04,099 --> 00:07:04,647
all along.

114
00:07:05,197 --> 00:07:06,932
One thing before you move on.

115
00:07:06,932 --> 00:07:11,836
Reread your PRD's threshold next to your golden set, the exact check 11.1 asked of

116
00:07:11,836 --> 00:07:12,913
the whole package.

117
00:07:12,913 --> 00:07:17,159
A mismatch here is the single most common gap in a first-pass capstone.

118
00:07:17,159 --> 00:07:21,944
Then add one case that specifically tries to break your feature, not just use it

119
00:07:21,944 --> 00:07:22,482
normally.

120
00:07:22,482 --> 00:07:27,446
If nothing comes to mind, borrow the shape of this lecture's boundary and injection

121
00:07:27,446 --> 00:07:27,745
rows.

122
00:07:27,745 --> 00:07:32,470
Next lecture is the worked solution, part two: cost, risk and metrics, the same

123
00:07:32,470 --> 00:07:34,503
feature, the other four artifacts.
